  "body": "Follow-up from #343 (per-setup machine and post).\n\n**Today (after #343):** a setup can own its machine and post (`SetupDef::machine` / `SetupDef::post`), but the app reads ONE mounted machine and post - the active setup's (`machine::of`, `machine::kind_of`, `posting::post_of`). Work that crosses mounts is refused by name (`camcraft_app::setup_mount`):\n- generating an operation whose setup mounts another machine or post than the active one (\"activate 'SetupN' first\");\n- posting a program whose setups disagree on machine or post (\"one program is written by one post for one machine\").\n\n**What it should be:**\n- Every machine reader that today asks `machine::kind_of(world)` / `machine::of(world)` (generation in `operation/job.rs`, `spindle_law::turns`, `rest.rs`, `setup_align::generation_problem`, `fixture::table_box`, the limit checker `check_document`, the op-creation family gate) takes the operation's setup, so an inactive setup's operations generate against their own machine without activating it - and the generation refusal goes away.\n- Posting a program whose setups use different posts writes one NC file per post (or per setup), each judged against its own machine, instead of refusing.\n\nDone means: two setups on a mill and a lathe generate without activation and post as two files, each byte-identical to posting that setup alone; the one-mount refusals are retired with tests proving the replacement.\n",
